<GuideLayout 
  title="The Science of Sweat: How Moisture-Wicking Technology Works" 
  type="TECH FABRIC"
  description="Understand the capillary action behind high-performance fabrics. Learn why moisture management is the #1 requirement for activewear."
>
  <h2>Why Moisture Wicking Matters</h2>
  <p>In high-intensity sports, sweat management isn't just about comfort—it's about performance. A garment that stays heavy and wet can lead to chafing, rapid body temperature drops, and decreased athlete focus.</p>
  
  <h3>The Capillary Action</h3>
  <p>Moisture-wicking fabrics are engineered to pull sweat away from the skin to the exterior of the fabric through a process called capillary action. The fibers are often cross-shaped or have specialized channels that increase surface area for faster evaporation.</p>

  <h3>Natural vs. Synthetic Wicking</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Synthetics (Nylon/Polyester):</strong> Inherently hydrophobic, meaning they don't absorb water. When treated with hydrophilic finishes, they move moisture extremely fast.</li>
    <li><strong>Natural Fibers (Bamboo/Cotton):</strong> Bamboo has natural wicking properties, but cotton absorbs moisture and holds it, making it less ideal for high-impact training.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Testing for Performance</h3>
  <p>At Fitclo, we conduct vertical wicking tests on all our performance fabrics. We ensure that our materials can move moisture at least 10cm within 30 minutes, keeping your customers dry during the most intense workouts.</p>
